g Requested). Ex Parte Communications: Please note that the Code of Judicial Conduct governing behavior by judges forbids the judges of the Eighth Judicial Circuit to discuss pending cases with the public. Please do not call or email the Court expecting to speak with a Judge about any case. If you have a comment to make, please mail it to the Clerk’s Office. Please keep in mind that the Court is only allowed to consider arguments made in court and documents filed by actual parties to the case. They cannot ethically read or consider any other opinions or arguments about the case, and they cannot give legal advice. Thank you. Unsolicited Communications: Unsolicited communications from non-parties will not be considered by the Court. Parties may only contact the judicial office in accordance with these practices and procedures. E-Filing Portal Contact Information: All attorneys and self-represented litigants must provide an e-mail address to receive signed orders electronically, unless excused. Fla. R. Gen. Prac. & Jud. Admin. 2.516. It is the responsibility of attorneys and self-represented litigants to update their contact information using Form 2.603 any time there is a change in the e-mail account registered for electronic service. E-Filing Website Scheduling Procedures Hearing Requests: You must use the hearing request form to email all hearing requests to AllenA@circuit8.org . Hearing requests are not accepted by telephone. Please confirm that your motion has been filed, served on all parties, and docketed prior to requesting hearing time. No hearing time will be given for motions that are not filed and docketed. If you have questions about whether your motion is filed and docketed, please contact the Alachua County Clerk of Court . When a motion is e-filed the Judge does not always receive a copy. If you file a motion that the Judge needs to see or act upon, you must provide a copy to the Judicial Assistant, AllenA@circuit8.org . All proposed orders must be submitted through the E-Portal. The notice of hearing shall include the full title of the motion and filing date, the length of the hearing, and shall advise if the hearing will be conducted in person or via Zoom. Once confirmation of the hearing date has been received by email from the judicial assistant, a notice of hearing shall be prepared by the moving party. If all parties may not receive notice of a hearing in sufficient time for the timeframe requested below, the Court may extend the timeframe for a matter to be heard. The Court will attempt to accommodate all requested timeframes but can make no assurance of availability on the Court’s calendar. Hearing Availability: It is mandatory that all parties consult with one another regarding their availability for hearing. If a hearing is unilaterally set without any effort to coordinate a date and time with opposing counsel, that matter will not be heard by the Court. However, if a party has made a good faith attempt to schedule a hearing with opposing counsel and counsel has failed to respond after three (3) attempts via email and/or phone, then the party may schedule the hearing unilaterally. Any hearings scheduled unilaterally pursuant to these instructions must include a note on the Notice of Hearing stating “Set Unilaterally Pursuant to Judge Van Vorst’s Divisional Instructions.” Further, counsel attending any hearing unilaterally set pursuant to these instructions will be expected to present proof of attempts to coordinate with opposing counsel at the hearing. Hearing Time: The moving party is responsible for requesting the appropriate amount of hearing time. Parties should presume that each side will be given an equal amount of time. If motions from multiple parties are set during the same hearing time, each moving party is responsible for requesting the appropriate amount of time. If there is a concern that a hearing may exceed the allotted time, either party may request for the Court to allot an equal amount of time to each party at the beginning of the hearing, or the Court may distribute time sua sponte. Parties will be responsible for presenting the entirety of their argument and presentation of evidence within their allotted time. Continuance Procedure: You must contact opposing counsel to determine if they have an objection prior to filing a Motion for Continuance. The Motion must be filed with the Clerk of Court and must indicate if opposing counsel objects to the continuance. If the continuance is agreed to by all parties, please submit an Agreed Order to the appropriate e-portal for the case’s division. If the opposing party objects to the continuance, it may be necessary to set a hearing on the Motion but still upload a proposed order to the appropriate e-portal for the case’s division.  Please see the procedure for scheduling a hearing. The Court will evaluate the Motions for Continuance on a case-by-case basis even if the opposing party agrees or does not object to the continuance. Cancelling Hearings: No hearing shall be cancelled unless the parties have the written agreement from the Court. Please notify the Court at least 24 hours in advance of any requests to cancel. Motions for Re-hearing/Reconsideration: Upon filing said motion, email a copy Court for review. The Court will either rule without a hearing or will advise the moving attorney to schedule a hearing. While an email may suffice in the correction of scrivener’s errors, please remember that emails pointing out errors do not toll the time for rehearing or reconsideration under the Rules. ID Requirement: If a party or witness will provide testimony at the scheduled hearing, the Court must receive by email to AllenA@circuit8.org , a photograph copy of their driver’s license or photo ID no later than three (3) business days prior to the hearing. Remote Appearance Motions for appearance by Zoom will be considered by the Court on a case-by-case basis.  A request for Zoom appearance may be denied.  If your client will appear in-person in court, a Zoom appearance by counsel will be denied.  In order to file a motion for Zoom appearance, you must have a fully operational camera.  Use of your camera is required for Zoom appearances. Please be advised that if your case is set on a block docket (such as pretrial conferences) and you are attending by Zoom, your case will be called after all other cases with in-person appearances are called. ALL MOTIONS FOR ZOOM APPEARANCE MUST BE FILED IN THE COURT FILE AT LEAST 3 BUSINESS DAYS PRIOR TO THE SCHEDULED COURT DATE.  A PROPOSED ORDER GRANTING THE ZOOM APPEARANCE MUST BE UPLOADED TO THE CASE’S DIVISION PORTAL AT LEAST 3 BUSINESS DAYS PRIOR TO THE SCHEDULED COURT DATE. FAILURE TO TIMELY FILE THE MOTION AND UPLOAD THE CORRECT ORDER TO THE EPORTAL, WILL RESULT IN DENIAL OF THE MOTION. THE COURT WILL PROVIDE THE ZOOM INFORMATION UPON APPROVAL OF THE COURT. Submission of Orders and Judgments Format for Proposed Orders: All proposed orders shall include the following coding after the body of the proposed order.  This coding allows our computer system to input the proper dates and signatures for processing. DONE AND ORDERED in Chambers at Gainesville, Alachua County, Florida on DDDD. JJJJ CERTIFICATE OF SERVICE I HEREBY CERTIFY that copies have been furnished by U.S. Mail or via filing with the Florida Courts E-Filing Portal on MMMM. CCCC AAAA DONE AND ORDERED in Chambers at Macclenny, Baker County , Florida on DDDD. JJJJ CERTIFICATE OF SERVICE I HEREBY CERTIFY that copies have been furnished by U.S. Mail or via filing with the Florida Courts E-Filing Portal on MMMM. CCCC AAAA Should an additional party or address be required for service of the order that is not contained in the court file, please type the name of the party who is to receive the order, as well as that party’s e-mail/e-service address OR mailing address at the end of the AAAA block in the Certificate of Service. Submission Method: Proposed orders shall be submitted via the Florida Courts E-Filing Portal ( www. https://www.myflcourtaccess.com/ ). Do not send proposed orders by United States Mail or email unless the proposed order is in PDF format. Please see the instructions above for formatting. If there are parties who have not consented to use of e-mail service, stamped envelopes should be mailed or hand delivered to the Judicial Assistant, Amber Allen. Please clip the envelopes together, along with a sticky note that indicates the case name and case number. PLEASE DO NOT ADDRESS THE ENVELOPES; LEAVE THE ENVELOPES BLANK BUT BE SURE TO ADD THE PROPER POSTAGE. Orders will not be entered until the motion has been docketed and imaged by the Clerk. Docketing and imaging by the Clerk may take more than 48 hours from electronic filing. Courtesy Copies of Case Law and Other Documents Cases being relied upon by the parties at a hearing or trial should be provided to opposing counsel and the Court no later than three (3) business days prior to the hearing. However, the Court will always consider any law applicable to the matters being litigated. Litigants should highlight the parts of a law or case that they believe are pertinent to the issues at hand. All other non-exhibit documents that a party wishes the Court to review must be provided to the Court and the opposing party by email to allena@circuit8.org at least three (3) business days prior to the hearing or trial. Each case or document should be provided as a separate file. Please confer with all parties prior to the hearing to determine whether there are any stipulations as to the admissibility of evidence. Any pre-marked evidence which is stipulated should be so noted. All other pre-marked evidence will be considered to be opposed. Emergency and Other Urgent Matters A copy of any “Emergency” motion e-filed with the Clerk of Court and served upon opposing counsel should be provided to the Judge’s office for review by emailing AllenA@circuit8.org .  If a hearing is requested, please follow the directions for Hearing Requests. If the Court determines that a hearing should be granted, the Court will advise the moving party of an expedited date and time for the hearing. A party requesting emergency action must include the words “Emergency Motion” in the title of the motion and must set forth in detail the nature of the emergency, the date by which a ruling is necessary, and the reason the ruling is needed by the stated date. The emergency motion process should only be used in situations where a child is threatened with harm or where a party plans to improperly remove a child from the state. See Loudermilk v. Loudermilk , 693 So. 2d 666 (Fla. 2d DCA 1997). If one of these circumstances exists or another that rises to the level that would require immediate action to prevent harm, a party may file an emergency motion. The unwarranted designation of a motion as an emergency motion may result in sanctions. All emergency motions must be verified. Emergency motions must be e-mailed to the judicial assistant for the Court’s review. If the motion fails to raise an actual emergency, it will be denied to the extent that it seeks emergency relief (but not as to the merits) and may be scheduled to be heard in the ordinary course. A party whose time-sensitive motion does not qualify as an emergency motion but who nonetheless requires an expedited ruling by a date certain may file an expedited motion. An expedited motion must include the words “Expedited Motion” in the title and must set forth in detail the date by which an expedited ruling is needed and the reason the ruling is needed by the stated date. As a general matter, an expedited motion is one that will become moot if not ruled upon by a date certain. Parties are reminded that difficulties with timesharing or other problems that are not objectively extremely serious and imminent are NOT emergencies. Exhibits for Evidentiary Proceedings If you intend to present any evidence or documents to the court at your hearing, you must pre-mark all items for identification (ex. “Petitioner’s Exhibit A, B, C..”) and serve a copy via Certified US Mail to all parties no less than ten (10) business days prior to the hearing. Digital Exhibits that cannot be sent via US Mail (i.e., videos, flash drives, etc.) must be included on the exhibit list (and properly marked) and hand delivered directly to the opposing counsel or party no later than ten (10) business days prior to the hearing. In addition, a paper copy of all pre-marked exhibits, along with an Index of Exhibits from each side, shall be received by the Court via Certified US Mail or by hand delivery to the Court’s Gainesville office no later than the deadline contained in your specific order, or five (5) business days prior to the hearing date. This packet will be used by the Court to stamp the official evidence as it is entered at the hearing/trial. The Court will not accept electronic devices (i.e., videos, flash drives, etc.) sent via US Mail; you may submit a physical copy at hearing/trial. Please confer with all parties prior to the hearing to determine whether there are any stipulations as to the admissibility of evidence. Any pre-marked evidence which is stipulated, should be so noted. All other pre-marked evidence will be considered opposed. Evidence should not be filed with the Clerk. Pretrial Procedures and Conferences All requests to set hearings must be emailed pursuant to the scheduling procedures above. Please be advised that filing a motion to set hearing must be emailed to the judicial assistant, AllenA@circuit8.org , and a hearing must be requested pursuant to the scheduling procedures. Setting Case for Trial or Final Hearing All requests to set trial or final hearing must be emailed pursuant to the scheduling procedures above. Please be advised that filing a motion to set trial or final hearing must be emailed to the judicial assistant, AllenA@circuit8.org , and a hearing must be requested pursuant to the scheduling procedures. Notices of Appearance, Motion to Withdraw and Substitution of Counsel Pursuant to Florida Rules Procedure, all motions to withdraw or for substitution of counsel must contain the client’s signature/consent and specific reason(s) for the withdraw/substitution. Additionally, all proposed orders granting withdrawal shall be accompanied by the filing of an email designation form for the client or notice to the Court that an email designation has been previously filed. If an attorney is unable to obtain client consent or an email designation filled out by the client, then the motion to withdraw or to substitute counsel may be set for hearing. Motions to withdraw filed after the case has been set for trial may not be granted, and all such orders shall include notice to the now pro se litigant of the date and time of the previously scheduled hearing or trial. Motions to withdraw, even with consent, may require a hearing if filed prior to an already scheduled hearing or trial. MOTIONS TO WITHDRAW AS THE ATTORNEY OF RECORD: When filing a Motion to Withdraw as Attorney of Record, you are required to include the client’s contact information, including address and e-mail address if any, within the text of the Motion and any proposed order on same. You are also required to serve on your client a copy of the Motion to Withdraw and a Notice of Hearing. You must include your client’s name and contact information in the certificate of service for both the Motion to Withdraw and the Notice of Hearing. Your proposed order must include your client’s contact information and indicate that your client has 30 days to retain new counsel and have counsel file a notice of appearance or your client shall be deemed to be proceeding pro-se unless the party must be represented per the rules of civil procedure. If you have your client’s written consent to withdraw there is no need to set your Motion to Withdraw for hearing. Instead, please file a consent to withdraw signed by the client in the court file.  Please submit your proposed order with the client’s contact information to the e-portal for the case division. SUBSTITUTION OF COUNSEL: Stipulations for substitution of counsel between law firms shall be signed by both the former attorney, the new attorney taking over the case and must include written client consent. The Stipulation shall be filed in the court file.  A proposed order may be submitted through the e-portal for the case division.  The Court will not grant a substitution of counsel unless client’s written consent is obtained. Administrative Order No. 1.30 – Attorney Appearance and Withdrawal Requirements for All Cases Forms, Equitable Distribution Spreadsheets, & Pretrial Statements Forms, Checklists, and Other Self-Help Resources for the 8th Judicial Circuit can be found at the Forms and Checklists page Consumer Information and Pamphlets from the Florida Bar (opens in new tab) Family Joint Pretrial Statement (docx) Equitable Distribution Spreadsheet v36 Equitable Distribution Spreadsheet Instructions Equitable Distribution Spreadsheet User Guide Other Division Procedures Administrative Order No. 5.09 – Standing Family Court Order Administrative Order No. 3.09 – Standing Case Management Order in County and Circuit Civil Cases ADA Accommodations: ADA Information Interpreter Requests: Interpreter Request Information Courts & Judges Court Calendars Jurors Departments & Services Self-Help Contact & Information Accessibility Policy Skip Navigation Courts & Judges Eighth Judicial Circuit About Administrative Judges Employment Circuit Judges Intranet (Restricted) Senior Judges Judicial Assignments General Magistrates and Hearing Officers Alachua County Baker County Bradford County Gilchrist County Levy County Union County About About About About About About Judiciary Judiciary Judiciary Judiciary Judiciary Judiciary Clerks Office Clerks Office Clerks Office Clerks Office Clerks Office Clerks Office Calendars Weekly Calendars